{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>מסיע</b>. Most of the commentators explained יסכן בם as 'grows warm through them,' and they said that its parallel is ותהי למלך סוכנת [and she became the king's stewardess] (1 Kings 1:2). But this meaning in Hebrew is not found in the light binyan [binyan kal], only as a participle standing [on its own]. The correct interpretation is that סוכנת means mistress of the king's treasuries, like ערי מסכנות [storage cities] (1 Kings 9:19), and its parallel is אל הסוכן הזה [to this steward] (Isaiah 22:15). But this interpretation has neither head nor tail, for what is its connection to 'if the iron is blunt' (Ecclesiastes 10:10)? Closer to me is that יסכן בם is from the root of סכנה [danger], and this is well attested in the words of the earlier authorities. The two verses are continuous and still in praise of wisdom, and it also adheres to the verse above: 'the fool through his folly breaches a hedge, but the wise man guards against this' (cf. Ecclesiastes 10:8). The meaning is that there is nothing in the world that a person can obtain without pain and toil and danger — even stones, which are at a person's disposal and whoever wishes may take them: he will strain in moving them to the place he wants. And likewise the trees of the forest: he will labor in splitting them, and sometimes he will come to danger."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Ecclesiastes 10:8 loosely rather than verbatim; rendered his paraphrase as his own words rather than as a direct citation."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:01.262422207Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}